{
  "schema": 1,
  "grade": "4",
  "band": "3-5",
  "focus": "Grade 4 is the year of precision and of informational-text language. Students stop at 'good enough' words and learn to choose the exact one: a verb of looking (gaze, squint, scan), a sense of a multiple-meaning word (volume, degree, yield), or the right member of a look-alike pair (advice/advise, farther/further). The strongest structural gains come from Latin roots (ject, vis, aud) and prefix and suffix families (inter-, sub-, non-, -able/-ible, -ness) that let students decode long content-area words. Cause-and-effect and sequence signal phrases (as a result, due to, thus, meanwhile) and the verbs of working with informational text (examine, distinguish, annotate, decode) give students the language to explain how and why. Compared with grade 3, words are more abstract and often come in word families; compared with grade 5, argument language, connotation, and figurative-language terms are still ahead.",
  "selection": "Selection started from words that recur across grade 4-6 informational and literary texts but are rarely explained in context (Tier 2), weighted toward words with productive word parts or several senses, since those pay back most when students meet unfamiliar vocabulary. High-frequency words most students already control were excluded, as were all words already assigned to grades K-3 and the words the grade 6-8 scope claims for later years (for example the transport/structure/inspect root families, cause-and-effect connectors such as therefore, and the analysis verbs interpret, illustrate, and demonstrate). Cross-curricular units name their Tier 3 words openly (friction, gravity, vibrate; colony, immigrant, government; angle, symmetry, perimeter; flashback, climax, point of view; rhyme, verse, haiku; projectile), never more than three per unit. Root units teach only roots that are real and productive in English (ject, vis, aud); Spanish cognates are favored throughout (visual/visual, audience/audiencia, project/proyecto, emergency/emergencia). Deliberately left to other grades: argument and evidence terms and connotation (grade 5), the roots tele, micro, scope, phon (grade 5), and Latin roots such as port, struct, and spect (grade 6).",
  "arc": [
    "Qualities That Make a Character",
